<!-- thread-media:psinet:end -->
<h3>From BBS user to employee</h3>
<p>By around <strong>1993</strong>, Nicchio was using SP Online, which would later become STI BBS and STI Internet. In 1994 or 1995, he visited the company's offices on Avenida Nove de Julho in São Paulo, partly out of curiosity and partly to seek sponsorship for his radio program. In 1999, Alexandre Godoi, the keyboardist in his band, was working at STI in Alphaville.</p>
<p>That same year, US-based PSINet acquired STI Internet, following other purchases of Brazilian providers. Its strategy was to rapidly assemble a national operation providing network infrastructure, hosting and corporate services. In November 1999, it announced plans to invest <strong>US$500 million</strong> in Brazilian infrastructure, in addition to further acquisitions.</p>
<p>Godoi introduced Nicchio to commercial manager Rogério Carulli, who hired him on <strong>June 1, 2000</strong>. Nicchio sold dedicated and shared servers, hosting, corporate internet access and private data lines. He initially worked in Alphaville and later in Tamboré. His employment lasted until <strong>May 2, 2001</strong>, when he resigned.</p>
<p>The transition from enthusiastic internet user to industry professional took place inside an organization designed for growth. Advertising, business services and a network of acquisitions supported expectations of expansion. Yet signs of the wider sector's crisis were beginning to appear in the office itself.</p>
<h3>Two indicators of the dot-com bubble</h3>
<p>In March 2000, PSINet shares had reached approximately <strong>US$60 on Nasdaq</strong>. The bursting bubble and the difficulties facing a company that had invested heavily in rapid expansion drove its value down. By early 2001, the price was approaching just a few cents.</p>
