</figure>
<h3>When the phones began to fall silent</h3>
<p>The chart shows one side of the crisis. The other was the commercial department. Phones that had once rung constantly began to fall silent. It did not happen overnight: inquiries gradually dwindled while the company's operations were still organized for expansion. This contrast became personal in Nicchio's account.</p>
<aside class="psinet-testimony" aria-label="Personal recollection">
<strong class="psinet-testimony__label">Personal recollection</strong>
<blockquote>
<p>“When I started at PSINet, the phone rang all the time. The commercial service team was incredibly busy. As the months went by, the calls dwindled. Toward the end, the phone hardly rang at all.</p>
<p>At the time, I had subscribed to Folha de S.Paulo and started taking the newspaper to work under my arm. Since there wasn't much to do, I'd read while the phone wasn't ringing. Some people thought it was an affront. I kept reading.</p>
<p>And we had another ritual: every day when we arrived at the office, we checked the Nasdaq to see what PSINet shares were worth. I watched the price fall into the cents. Meanwhile, the silence in the office just kept growing.”</p>
</blockquote>
</aside>
<h3>The end of a chapter</h3>
<p>On <strong>April 3, 2001</strong>, Nasdaq suspended trading in PSINet shares, then close to US$0.19; formal delisting followed on April 27. Nicchio left the company on May 2. In June, the US parent sought protection under <em>Chapter 11</em>. The Brazilian operation announced its closure on <strong>August 20, 2001</strong>.</p>
<p>The story combines two perspectives rarely found together: a participant who witnessed Brazil's early BBS services evolve into commercial providers and an employee who experienced the collapse of an internet multinational from the inside. Nasdaq losses were numbers; the quiet phones made the crisis part of everyday life.</p>
<p>The dedicated page preserves the complete chronology of acquisitions, corporate services, daily work, the unfolding crisis and surviving photographs. This vertical offers the essential story, together with the original recollection, the market chart and archival images that make it tangible.</p>
